# scan-runner-pod-lifecycle.service — periodically scan the k3s runner node for
# the runner-pod LIFECYCLE stall (scan-runner-pod-lifecycle.sh): PVCs stuck
# Pending, scheduler volume-bind expiries, containerd inotify exhaustion,
# containerd create-path deadlines and teardown starvation, the ARC hook's
# failure signature and its precursor (and, from the listener archive, jobs
# that failed before any step could run), stale scale-set listeners, a runner
# node missing the ci-runner.io/churn-slot capacity every queue is denominated
# in, the warm cache over budget or its populator stalled, a job start's seed
# cost, and the API server itself going away (k3s restarts, refused reads).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: every member of this family was found by a human reading a
# red check whose name misdirected — a `check-format: FAILURE` for a check
# that never ran, an `Initialize containers` error on an unrelated PR — minutes
# to hours after the host had started failing to bring pods up, with every
# capacity signal and the wedged-runner sweep reporting nothing wrong
# (livespec plan ci-runner-pod-lifecycle-reliability, epic livespec-ifwnqj).
# A timer is the same pragmatic, homelab-scale answer this tree already uses
# for the wedged-runner sweep (../wedged-runner/) and the churn-slot reapply.
#
# REPORT-ONLY BY DESIGN — there is no clear mode and so no MODE_PLACEHOLDER
# here (see install-runner-pod-lifecycle-scan.sh's header for why). The unit
# lands in `failed` whenever a class is present, so `systemctl is-failed
# scan-runner-pod-lifecycle.service` and this unit's journal are the signal.
#
# THE DEADLINE (livespec-kgdlte). On 2026-09-06 05:55Z a sweep's kubectl
# child hung six minutes with no socket open while the API answered; with no
# TimeoutStartSec the oneshot stayed `activating` and the timer's cadence
# stopped silently until the child was killed by hand. TimeoutStartSec=240
# keeps every sweep under the 5-minute timer; the same number is passed in
# as RPL_DEADLINE_SECONDS so the script can shrink its per-step deadline to
# what is left and finish with a report instead of being killed mid-sweep.
# HOW TO TELL A KILL FROM A FINDING: a finding ends the journal with a
# `RUNNER-POD LIFECYCLE STALL` line and `systemctl show -p Result` reads
# `exit-code`; a deadline kill ends it with the script's `KILLED: SIGTERM`
# line (or, if even that was cut short, with systemd's own `start operation
# timed out. Terminating.`) and Result reads `timeout`. Neither `failed`
# alone nor the absence of a CLEAN line is a stall. TimeoutStopSec=30 bounds
# the kill itself: SIGTERM at 240 s, SIGKILL at 270 s, so the unit is out of
# `deactivating` before the 300 s timer tick even when the child is wedged
# in a syscall (a D-state read on a stalled array survives TERM; the unit
# still leaves the timer's way — the KILL lands when the syscall returns).
[Unit]
Description=Scan the runner node for the runner-pod lifecycle stall (report-only)
After=k3s.service
Requires=k3s.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=KUBECONFIG=/etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml
Environment=RPL_DEADLINE_SECONDS=240
TimeoutStartSec=240
TimeoutStopSec=30
ExecStart=/usr/local/lib/ci-runner-k3s/scan-runner-pod-lifecycle.sh
